        "scanners": {
          "vt": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "analysis": "Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: baoyu-post-to-weibo\nVersion: 1.76.3\n\nThe skill bundle provides sophisticated Weibo automation but employs several high-risk behaviors and potential vulnerabilities. It uses AppleScript (macOS) and PowerShell (Windows) to simulate system-level keystrokes and manipulate the clipboard to bypass bot detection (found in `paste-from-clipboard.ts` and `copy-to-clipboard.ts`). The `SKILL.md` file contains prompt-injection-style instructions directing the AI agent to autonomously terminate Chrome processes (`pkill`) without user confirmation. Furthermore, `scripts/vendor/baoyu-md/src/utils/languages.ts` performs dynamic imports of JavaScript from a third-party CDN (cdn-doocs.oss-cn-shenzhen.aliyuncs.com), and `paste-from-clipboard.ts` is vulnerable to AppleScript injection via the `--app` argument. While these features support the stated goal of browser automation, they grant the agent excessive system control and introduce remote code execution risks.",
            "source": "palm",
            "checkedAt": 1775971973485
          },
          "skillspector": null,
          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "medium",
            "summary": "The skill's files and instructions largely match a Weibo posting tool, but it accesses local Chrome profiles and invokes host utilities that are not fully declared, so there are coherent uses but some unexplained / under-documented privileges you should understand before installing.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "The name/description (post to Weibo, support Markdown articles via Chrome CDP) aligns with the included scripts: markdown conversion, CDP helpers, clipboard/paste utilities and weibo-specific post logic. Reusing a logged-in Chrome profile and using CDP is expected for this purpose.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "Runtime instructions direct the agent to run TypeScript scripts in the skill's scripts/ folder which discover and read Chrome user-data directories (DevToolsActivePort) and may parse process lists. They also rely on system clipboard and synthetic keystroke tools (osascript, swift, xdotool, ydotool, wl-copy/xclip, powershell). Those actions go beyond simple HTTP API calls and access local browser state (cookies, sessions), which is necessary to reuse an already-authenticated session but expands the data surface and host-level capabilities.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "No install spec (instruction-only). Code files are bundled and executed via bun or npx as described. No network-download install URLs were used, which lowers supply-chain risk — but scripts use npx -y bun (which may invoke remote package tool behavior) and spawn system commands at runtime.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The skill declares no required env vars or credentials, which is consistent with using an existing Chrome session, but the code reads environment variables opportunistically (overrides for Chrome path/profile) and probes standard Chrome user-data locations. It also expects external system utilities (swift, osascript, xdotool, ydotool, wl-copy/xclip, powershell) that are not listed in the skill's declared required binaries — this mismatch is an under-documentation risk.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "concern"
